{
  "primary_meaning": "The Garden of Eden as paradise: the original place of perfect creation, innocence, abundance, and unhindered communion with God, later associated with the Fall and eschatological restoration",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Genesis 2:8–15 (planting of Eden, trees of life and knowledge, rivers, Adam's placement)",
    "Genesis 3 (temptation, Fall, expulsion from the garden)",
    "Revelation 2:7 and 22:1–5 (tree of life and paradise of God restored in the new creation)"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "The Church (or the soul of the believer) as an enclosed, cultivated garden of divine love and fruitfulness",
      "basis": [
        "Song of Songs 4:12–16; 5:1; 6:2 (enclosed garden, fountain, spices)",
        "Patristic and medieval allegories equating the bride/garden with the Church or soul"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Gethsemane as the place of agony, prayer, submission to the Father's will, and betrayal",
      "basis": [
        "Matthew 26:36–46; Mark 14:32–42; Luke 22:39–46; John 18:1 (Jesus' prayer and arrest in the garden)"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Spiritual growth or the cultivated life of virtue under God's care",
      "basis": [
        "Isaiah 58:11; Jeremiah 31:12 (souls as watered gardens)",
        "Parables and imagery of planting, pruning, and fruitfulness (e.g., John 15)"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "General fertility, life, or the created order under divine blessing (without specific Edenic or ecclesial reference)",
      "basis": [
        "Genesis 1–2 creation motifs; various poetic uses of gardens/orchards in wisdom and prophetic literature"
      ],
      "prominence": "rare"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "In mainstream Christian biblical interpretation and teaching the term 'Garden' most immediately and commonly evokes Eden as paradise lost and restored; other readings (ecclesial, Gethsemane, spiritual-cultivation) are well-attested but secondary or context-specific. No major tradition treats the term as lacking symbolic content."
}
